The '''Twelve Guarantees''' ({{langx|ca|Las Doce Garantías}}) are a set of twelve constitutional provisions that form the legal foundation of the post-reunification Balboan state. Drafted by the [[Adonian Community]] legal office in consultation with the [[Sant Cristòfor]] Consultative Assembly and presented to [[Balboa]] as a non-negotiable condition of the return of the mandate territory in 1960, the Guarantees establish full civil rights, anti-discrimination protections, reparations to descendants of enslaved persons, and a framework of international oversight. They are entrenched in the [[Constitution of Balboa]] and may not be altered or repealed without the consent of the Adonian Community.

The Guarantees were the product of the [[No Return movement]] and the broader political mobilisation of the non-white population of Sant Cristòfor during the [[International Mandate of Sant Cristòfor|AC mandate period]] (1950–1960). The movement, which opposed the return of the mandate territory to Balboan sovereignty without legal protections for the Black, mixed, and Fosian populations, forced the Adonian Community to impose binding conditions on the transfer. The Guarantees have been the subject of political controversy since their imposition, with Balboan nationalists regarding them as infringements on sovereignty and the non-white communities regarding them as essential safeguards.

== Background ==

{{main|International Mandate of Sant Cristòfor|No Return movement}}

The [[International Mandate of Sant Cristòfor]] was established in 1950 following the dissolution of the [[Zong Balboa]] administration after the [[Great Adonian War]]. The mandate, administered directly by the Adonian Community, was charged with the reconstruction of Sant Cristòfor, the resettlement of displaced populations, and the determination of the territory's permanent status. By the late 1950s, the reconstruction was substantially complete, and the question of the territory's future had become the central political issue.

The [[No Return movement]], which emerged in 1952, opposed the return of the mandate territory to Balboa without legal protections for the non-white population. The movement drew its support from the Black Balboan majority, which had experienced racial oppression under the [[Balboa Boba]] — the white planter oligarchy that governed the rump state — and which feared that a return to Balboan sovereignty would mean a return to the pre-war racial hierarchy. The movement organised mass demonstrations, including a protest of 100,000 residents in 1958, and forced the Adonian Community to confront the question of what conditions would be imposed on the return.

The Adonian Community, which had no desire to preside over a territory in open revolt, determined that the return would be conditional upon Balboa's acceptance of a set of binding guarantees. The Guarantees were drafted by the AC legal office in consultation with the Sant Cristòfor Consultative Assembly and were presented to Balboa as a non-negotiable condition of the transfer. Balboa accepted the Guarantees under considerable diplomatic pressure, though the Balboan government regarded several of the provisions — particularly the reparations clause, the AC enforcement mechanism, and the creation of the [[International City of Areza]] — as infringements on its sovereignty.

== Provisions ==

The Twelve Guarantees, as set out in the [[Treaty of Balboa Harbour]] (1960), are as follows:

=== First Guarantee: Full civil rights ===

Full legal civil rights for all races, including the right to vote, to hold office, and to own property without racial restriction. This guarantee abolished the racial restrictions on voting and property ownership that had been maintained by the rump state and that had been a central feature of the [[Balboa Boba]]'s political system. The guarantee was extended to all persons within Balboan territory, regardless of race, ethnicity, or national origin.

=== Second Guarantee: Anti-discrimination ===

Statutory prohibition on discrimination in employment, housing, and commercial activity, enforceable by independent courts. This guarantee established the legal framework for anti-discrimination enforcement that has been the basis for subsequent labour law, housing law, and commercial regulation. The [[Balboan Labour Authority]], established in 1963, was the first institution created to enforce this guarantee. The anti-discrimination provisions have been most effectively enforced in [[Estret Province]], where the canal economy and the presence of international institutions have created a strong compliance culture; enforcement in the interior provinces has been less consistent.

=== Third Guarantee: Non-white representation ===

Guaranteed representation of non-white peoples in the Balboan national legislature, with a minimum number of seats allocated to the former mandate territory. This guarantee ensured that the Black and mixed populations of Sant Cristòfor and the former mandate territory would have a voice in the national legislature regardless of the electoral outcomes in the rest of the country. The minimum seat allocation has been adjusted periodically through constitutional amendments, though the principle of guaranteed representation has been maintained.

=== Fourth Guarantee: Zong and Creole education ===

The right to maintain [[Zong language|Zong]]-language and [[Balboan Creole language|Balboan Creole]]-language education in public schools. This guarantee protected the linguistic heritage of the Zong and Creole-speaking communities, which had been established during the Zong Balboa period and the mandate era. The Zong-language school system, which had been a central institution of the Zong Balboa administration, was preserved under the Guarantees as a public educational system open to all students regardless of ethnicity.

=== Fifth Guarantee: Multilingual publication ===

The right to maintain publications in Zong, Balboan Creole, and [[Sinchewese language|Sinchewese]] without censorship. This guarantee protected the press and publishing traditions of the multilingual communities of Sant Cristòfor, which had produced significant bodies of literature, journalism, and scholarship during the Zong Balboa and mandate periods. The guarantee has been cited in subsequent legal challenges to government attempts to regulate media content.

=== Sixth Guarantee: Reparations ===

{{main|Balboan Land and Reparations Authority}}

Reparations to [[Black Balboan people|Black Balboans]] who were descendants of enslaved persons, in the form of land grants, educational scholarships, and capital endowments for community institutions. The reparations programme was one of the most contentious provisions of the Guarantees and was regarded by the Balboan government as the most significant infringement on its sovereignty. The programme was administered by the [[Balboan Land and Reparations Authority]], which operated from 1962 to 1998. The land grants formed the basis of the cooperative farm system, which replaced the white-owned plantations; the educational scholarships provided access to secondary and post-secondary education for the Black community; and the capital endowments supported the establishment of community banks, credit unions, and cultural institutions.

The reparations programme has been the subject of ongoing debate. Supporters argue that it was a necessary acknowledgement of the injustice of slavery and the racial hierarchy of the rump state; critics argue that the payments were insufficient to close the wealth gap and that the cooperative farm system, while providing security, has not generated the income growth needed to lift rural Black communities out of poverty.

=== Seventh Guarantee: AC enforcement ===

Full Adonian Community enforcement of all provisions: the AC retained the right to challenge Balboan laws and administrative actions that violated the Guarantees before the [[AC Tribunal]]. This guarantee established the Adonian Community as the ultimate arbiter of the Guarantees and gave the AC the legal authority to intervene in Balboan domestic affairs when the Guarantees were violated. The enforcement mechanism has been invoked on several occasions, most notably in the 1970s and 1980s, when the AC Tribunal ruled against Balboan government attempts to restrict the scope of the anti-discrimination provisions. The guarantee has been the most persistent source of friction between Balboa and the Adonian Community, as it places a limit on Balboan sovereignty that cannot be removed without AC consent.

=== Eighth Guarantee: Canal oversight ===

{{main|Balboa Canal|AC Canal Commission}}

The continued operation of the [[Balboa Canal]] under international oversight. This guarantee ensured that the canal, which had been the subject of international conflict since the [[Second War of the Isthmus]], would remain under international supervision and could not be used as an instrument of Balboan national policy. The [[AC Canal Commission]], established by the [[Treaty of Areza]] (1973), oversees the canal's operation and ensures that it remains open to international traffic on non-discriminatory terms. The canal is operated by the [[Balboa Canal Authority]], a Balboan state agency, under the commission's oversight.

=== Ninth Guarantee: International Cooperative Police Force ===

{{main|International Cooperative Police Force}}

The establishment of an [[International Cooperative Police Force]] (ICPF) to manage disputes in Balboa and to ensure the enforcement of the Guarantees, operating under AC authority. The ICPF was composed of officers seconded from AC member states and from the Balboan national police, operating under AC command authority. Its mandate included the investigation of civil rights violations, the mediation of intercommunal disputes, and the provision of security for institutions established under the Guarantees. The ICPF operated throughout Balboa, not only in the former mandate territory, and its presence was a source of persistent friction between Balboa and the Adonian Community. The ICPF's mandate in Balboa proper was terminated in the 1990s as part of a phased reduction of AC oversight following Balboa's demonstrated compliance with the Guarantees. The ICPF's mandate in [[International City of Areza|Areza]] is perpetual.

=== Tenth Guarantee: International City of Areza ===

{{main|International City of Areza}}

The creation of the [[International City of Areza]], carved from the canal zone, as a permanently neutral international territory governed by the Adonian Community. Areza, situated on the north shore of [[Lake Alvarado]], serves as the administrative seat of the AC's oversight of the Balboa Canal and the Twelve Guarantees. The city is independent of Balboa: it maintains its own governance structures, its own military base (the [[AC Garrison Areza]]), its own tribunals, and its own legal system. The [[AC Parliament]] is permanently seated in Areza. Areza's status is guaranteed in perpetuity: it is not subject to Balboan law, cannot be incorporated into Balboan territory, and is administered by the AC without an expiry date.

=== Eleventh Guarantee: Right of petition ===

The right of the former mandate population to petition the AC directly regarding violations of the Guarantees. This guarantee provided a direct channel for individuals and communities to raise concerns about the implementation of the Guarantees without going through the Balboan government. The petition mechanism has been used extensively, particularly in the first decades after the reunification, when the enforcement of the anti-discrimination provisions was still being established. The AC receives and investigates petitions through its legal office in Areza.

=== Twelfth Guarantee: Entrenchment ===

The prohibition on the Balboan state from altering or repealing the Guarantees without the consent of the Adonian Community. This guarantee entrenches the Guarantees as a permanent feature of the Balboan constitutional order and ensures that no Balboan government, regardless of its parliamentary majority, can unilaterally modify or abolish the protections established by the Guarantees. The entrenchment provision has been the most controversial aspect of the Guarantees, as it places a permanent limit on Balboan sovereignty that cannot be removed through domestic political processes.

== Implementation ==

The implementation of the Guarantees was carried out over the three decades following the reunification. The [[Balboan People's Party]] (BPP) government, which came to power in the first post-reunification elections, was broadly committed to the Guarantees and established the institutional framework for their enforcement. The [[Balboan Labour Authority]] (1963), the [[Balboan Land and Reparations Authority]] (1962–1998), and the [[Balboan Investment Authority]] were all created under the Guarantees framework.

The land reform programme, mandated by the Sixth Guarantee, was the most significant implementation measure. The large plantations, which had been owned primarily by white Balboan families, were expropriated with compensation and reorganised as agricultural cooperatives, to which former plantation workers were granted shares. The reparations programme provided direct payments, educational scholarships, and capital endowments to descendants of enslaved persons. Both programmes were administered by the Balboan Land and Reparations Authority.

The anti-discrimination provisions of the Second Guarantee were implemented through a series of legislative acts in the 1960s and 1970s, which established the legal framework for non-discrimination in employment, housing, and commercial activity. The Balboan Labour Authority was given the power to investigate complaints, issue enforcement orders, and refer cases to the courts. Enforcement has been most effective in Estret Province, where the presence of international institutions and the canal economy have created a strong compliance culture; enforcement in the interior provinces has been less consistent.

== Political debate ==

The Twelve Guarantees have been a central issue in Balboan politics since their imposition. Three broad positions have characterised the debate:

The [[Balboan People's Party|BPP]] has consistently supported the Guarantees as the foundation of the post-reunification order and has defended the entrenchment provision as necessary to protect the rights of the non-white population. The BPP has argued that the Guarantees have contributed to a stable investment climate and have established the rule of law that underpins the canal economy.

The [[Democratic Alliance]] has called for a renegotiation of the Guarantees, arguing that the entrenchment provision and the AC enforcement mechanism are outdated and that Balboa, as a sovereign state and a member of the Adonian Community, should be trusted to maintain civil rights protections without external oversight. The Alliance has not proposed the abolition of the Guarantees but has argued that the most intrusive provisions — the AC enforcement mechanism, the ICPF, and the entrenchment clause — should be phased out as Balboa demonstrates its commitment to the Guarantees' principles.

The [[Socialist Front]] has defended the Guarantees but has argued that they have not gone far enough. The Front has called for the expansion of the reparations programme, the strengthening of the anti-discrimination provisions, and the extension of the cooperative model into non-agricultural sectors. The Front has also opposed any weakening of the AC enforcement mechanism, arguing that the Guarantees' protections are only as strong as the external enforcement that sustains them.

The [[Balboan Sovereignty Movement]], a smaller nationalist formation, has called for the complete abrogation of the Guarantees, arguing that they represent an unacceptable infringement on Balboan sovereignty. The movement has not gained significant electoral support, as the Guarantees remain popular among the non-white majority, but its arguments have influenced the Democratic Alliance's position on renegotiation.

== Legal challenges ==

The Guarantees have been the subject of several significant legal challenges before the [[AC Tribunal]]. In the 1970s, the Balboan government attempted to restrict the scope of the anti-discrimination provisions to the former mandate territory, arguing that the Second Guarantee applied only to Sant Cristòfor and not to the interior provinces. The AC Tribunal ruled that the Guarantees applied throughout Balboan territory and that the anti-discrimination provisions were of general application. This ruling established the principle that the Guarantees were national, not regional, in scope.

In the 1980s, a challenge was brought against the Balboan government's attempt to limit the reparations programme to the descendants of persons enslaved within Balboa, excluding the descendants of persons enslaved in other Kaftian territories who had been transported to Balboa. The AC Tribunal ruled that the Sixth Guarantee's reference to "Black Balboans who were descendants of enslaved persons" was to be interpreted broadly and that the reparations programme could not exclude persons who had been enslaved elsewhere and transported to Balboa.

In the 1990s, the phased reduction of the ICPF's mandate in Balboa proper was challenged by a coalition of Black Balboan community organisations, which argued that the reduction was premature and that the enforcement of the Guarantees remained insufficient to justify the withdrawal of international oversight. The AC Tribunal ruled that the reduction was consistent with the Guarantees, as the Ninth Guarantee provided for the ICPF's mandate to be adjusted in accordance with Balboa's demonstrated compliance, and that the ICPF's mandate in Areza remained perpetual.

== Legacy ==

The Twelve Guarantees have shaped every aspect of the post-reunification Balboan state. The civil rights and anti-discrimination provisions established the legal framework for the integration of the non-white population into the political and economic life of the country. The reparations programme, while insufficient to close the wealth gap, provided the basis for the cooperative farm system and the Black Balboan educational and institutional infrastructure. The AC enforcement mechanism and the entrenchment provision have ensured that the Guarantees cannot be diluted or removed through domestic political processes, providing a degree of constitutional stability that has contributed to the country's investment climate and its reputation for rule of law.

The Guarantees have also been a source of persistent tension between Balboa and the Adonian Community. The entrenchment provision, which places a permanent limit on Balboan sovereignty, has been the most controversial aspect, and the question of whether and when the Guarantees might be renegotiated remains an open issue in Balboan politics. The creation of the International City of Areza, which is permanently independent of Balboa, has been a particular point of friction, as it represents a permanent international presence on Balboan territory that cannot be removed without AC consent.

The Guarantees have been studied extensively as a model of post-conflict constitutional engineering and as a case study in the use of international enforcement mechanisms to protect minority rights. They have been compared to the constitutional protections established in other post-conflict states, though the combination of domestic entrenchment with external enforcement is unusual and has been the subject of debate in the literature on constitutional design.

